# Data Fetching & State Management Flow

## PURPOSE

Define a consistent, scalable, and secure approach to:

* fetching data
* managing state
* interacting with backend services

This document is framework-agnostic and reusable across projects.

---

# LAYERING (Separation of Concerns)

System must follow strict layering:

UI → Hook/Container → Data Layer → Transport → Backend Client

---

## 1. UI COMPONENTS

Responsibilities:

* render data
* handle user interactions (events)
* receive data and callbacks via props

Rules:

* NO direct API calls
* NO business logic
* NO knowledge of transport/backend
* must remain pure and reusable

---

## 2. SCREEN / CONTAINER HOOK

Responsibilities:

* orchestrate data flow
* read route params / inputs
* trigger queries and mutations
* map raw data → view model

Rules:

* may combine multiple data sources
* may transform data for UI
* must NOT call transport directly

---

## 3. DATA ACCESS LAYER

Responsibilities:

* define queries and mutations
* provide typed interfaces
* normalize inputs
* define cache behavior

Examples:

* React Query hooks
* RTK Query endpoints
* custom API hooks

Rules:

* all data fetching starts here
* no UI logic
* no direct DOM interaction
* must be fully typed

---

## 4. TRANSPORT BOUNDARY (CRITICAL)

The ONLY layer allowed to:

* know backend base URLs
* handle authentication (tokens/cookies)
* access server-side secrets

---

### BFF / PROXY MODEL (REQUIRED)

Client must call:

/api/...

Server-side handlers:

* call backend services
* inject auth
* enforce security

---

### STRICT RULE

❌ Direct-to-backend calls from client are forbidden
(no exceptions)

---

### MINIMUM REQUIREMENTS

* timeout must be set for every request
* correlation/request ID must be propagated
* all outbound calls must be logged (at least minimally)

---

## 5. BACKEND CLIENT

Responsibilities:

* typed HTTP communication
* request/response shaping

Examples:

* OpenAPI generated client
* Axios wrapper
* fetch wrapper

Rules:

* used only behind transport boundary
* must be typed
* must not leak transport concerns

---

# STATE MANAGEMENT STRATEGY

## SERVER STATE (Preferred for fetched data)

Use query cache (React Query / RTK Query):

* store API responses
* reuse cached data
* avoid duplication

Rules:

* do NOT copy server data into global store unnecessarily
* cache key must include ALL inputs

---

## CLIENT STATE (App-owned state)

Store only what belongs to UI/app:

* selected IDs
* filters
* form drafts
* UI preferences
* optimistic updates

---

## DERIVED STATE

Rules:

* do NOT store derived data
* compute via selectors/memoization

Examples:

* filtered lists
* sorted lists
* counts
* active entity

---

### EXCEPTION

Derived state may be stored only if:

* computation is expensive
* needed for offline mode
* needed for audit/logging

---

# FETCH STRATEGY

* Fetch data at screen/container level
* Avoid fetching in leaf components
* Avoid duplicate requests for same data
* Prefer cached data over refetching

---

# STABLE KEYING (CRITICAL)

Same logical request → same cache key

---

## NORMALIZATION RULES

* Arrays → sort if order doesn't matter
* Strings → trim / normalize casing
* Empty/null/undefined → unify representation
* Numbers → enforce consistent type
* Objects → use deterministic subset of fields
* Scope → include user/tenant context

---

# MUTATIONS & INVALIDATION

Rules:

* mutations must invalidate or update related queries
* keep cache consistent
* avoid stale data

---

## STRATEGIES

* refetch after mutation
* manual cache update
* optimistic updates

---

# OPTIMISTIC UPDATES

* apply changes immediately in UI
* rollback on failure
* keep UX responsive

---

# ERROR & LOADING MODEL

Expose consistent state:

* data
* isLoading
* isError
* error

---

## RULES

* do NOT swallow errors silently
* map backend errors → user-safe format
* avoid leaking internal details

---

# TYPE SAFETY

* all boundaries must be typed
* no `any` across layers
* validate incoming data when needed

---

# AI INTEGRATION RULES

If system uses AI:

* AI calls must go through data layer
* never call AI directly from UI
* validate and parse AI responses
* do NOT expose raw AI output to UI

---

# SECURITY RULES

* tokens must never reach client components
* backend URLs must never be exposed
* secrets only in transport/server layer

---

# PERFORMANCE RULES

* avoid unnecessary network calls
* generate/fetch only what is needed
* lazy-load heavy data when possible

---

# OBSERVABILITY

* include correlation/request ID
* log key request parameters
* track failures and latency

---

# DEFINITION OF DONE (CHECKLIST)

* [ ] No direct API calls from UI
* [ ] Data layer handles all requests
* [ ] Transport boundary isolates backend logic
* [ ] Types defined across all layers
* [ ] Cache key includes all inputs
* [ ] Inputs normalized
* [ ] Mutation invalidation handled
* [ ] Error/loading states implemented
* [ ] Timeout configured
* [ ] Correlation ID propagated
* [ ] No secrets in client code

---

# FINAL PRINCIPLE

Data flow must be:

* predictable
* secure
* scalable
* easy to reason about

If unsure:

→ choose the simplest correct approach
→ keep layers clean
→ avoid shortcuts that break architecture
